# Sensory-friendly interaction

Design for **sensory sensitivity** alongside play-first UX. Pair with [child-experience.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-experience.mdc), [visual-design.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/visual-design.mdc), [little-adventure-world.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/little-adventure-world.mdc) (ambient world motion limits), and [child-ui-components.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-ui-components.mdc) when implementing child-facing UI.

## Avoid

- Flashing elements or strobing (including rapid opacity or colour toggles)
- Rapid animation or high-frequency motion
- Sudden large movements (elements jumping across the screen without clear cause)
- Unexpected audio (sounds that play without a clear, repeatable user action)
- Loud error sounds or harsh alert tones
- Excessive confetti, particle bursts, or stacked celebration effects
- Constant background motion (parallax loops, always-on wiggling decorations, busy ambient animation)

Illustrated **static** environment assets are preferred for calm scenes; if motion is added to world layers later, keep it **optional, subtle, and off** under reduced motion — same rules as above.

Walk Through the Week storybook banners and integrated UI should use **subtle** depth only — no aggressive shadows, flashing voice borders, or dense chrome that competes with the illustrated environment.

## Animations should be

- **Short** — brief enough that the child can return to the task quickly
- **Predictable** — motion follows obvious cause and effect (tap → gentle response)
- **Purposeful** — every animation communicates state or feedback; no decoration-only motion in the play loop
- **Easy to understand** — one focal motion at a time; avoid many simultaneous effects

Success animations should feel **rewarding without being overwhelming** — a small bounce, glow, or sparkle is enough; scale duration and intensity down before adding more effects.

## Audio

- Audio must always be **optional and controllable** (mute / volume in a **parent/guardian** area, not cluttering the child play path).
- Default to **soft** levels; no autoplay on screen entry unless the product explicitly allows it and parents can disable it.
- Pair sound with **visual** feedback so meaning is clear when sound is off.

## Meaning beyond colour

Do **not** rely on colour alone to communicate meaning (correct vs incorrect, selected vs unselected, enabled vs disabled).

- Combine colour with **shape, icon, position, border, or motion** so state is obvious without reading.
- Respect contrast enough for common colour-vision differences; use tokens, not one-off “red = bad”-only patterns in child UI.

## Consistent interaction patterns

Prefer **the same gestures and feedback** across activities (tap to choose, drag to match, same success “feel”) so the child learns how the interface behaves.

- No surprise controls or one-off interaction models per mini-game unless the activity truly requires it — and then teach it visually once, consistently.
- Honor **`prefers-reduced-motion`**: keep essential state visible (icons, highlights) when motion is reduced; see [child-ui-components.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/child-ui-components.mdc).
